Welcome to the Lisaac Ω world !

Lisaac Ω is a prototype-based, object-oriented programming language, designed to teach
programming on solid foundations, from the very first steps up to professional software.
- Safe typing, checked at compile time, and design by contract (pre- and postconditions).
- Compiled to portable C, then to native code: the same program runs on Linux, Windows,
  macOS, Android and in a web browser (WebAssembly), with no compromise on performance.
- A consistent notation, close to mathematics: even `if' and `while' are ordinary
  methods of the library, not keywords.
- A graphical editor, elit, which displays the code with true mathematical symbols.
- Free software and free of charge.

Lisaac Ω is developed at the Université de Strasbourg, by Benoît Sonntag, who is the
most important author, and Dominique Colnet from the Université de Lorraine
(IUT Nancy-Charlemagne).

Web site: https://lisaac.org

Installation

From this directory (lisaac), run:

  ./install.sh

It first checks the few system packages needed by the graphics and the sound, and
proposes to install the missing ones (apt on Linux, brew on macOS, pacman in the
"MSYS2 UCRT64" terminal on Windows). Then it compiles the compiler, and opens the
graphical installer (itself written in Lisaac Ω), where you choose the parts to
install: elit, elito, the calculator, the Logo turtle, the games.
./install.sh --all installs everything without question; ./install.sh --text stays in
the terminal (this is automatic when there is no display).
In case of trouble, add the --verbose option.

Everything stays in this directory: no administrator rights, no system file touched.
Outside of it, only one line of your shell startup file (~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc) is
added, after "LISAAC-OMEGA TOOLS", to put lisaac/bin in your PATH. To uninstall, delete
this directory and that line.

Keep this directory where it is, and keep its name "lisaac".
If you move it, run ./install.sh again from its new location.


Two commands

The installation leaves its executables in lisaac/bin, which is added to your PATH.
Two commands are enough for almost everything:
- elit, the code editor and viewer;
- lisaac, the command-line compiler.

To read a program, give elit the full name of the file:

  elit example/hello_world.li

To compile it and run it:

  lisaac example/hello_world.li
  ./hello_world

During development, compile without options: all the contracts are checked.
For a much smaller and faster executable, add -boost (and -q for a quiet compilation):

  lisaac example/hello_world -q -boost

All the options are listed by: lisaac -help


Guided tours

Each of these programs opens a window that shows a series of examples, with their code:

  big_show                                    #  A general tour of Lisaac Ω.
  tortue_show                                 #  The Logo turtle, for beginners (in French).
  game_show                                   #  The games (see below).
  cd example && lisaac example_show && ./example_show

An example compiled at the installation (the drawings of the turtle, the games) starts
at once; the others are compiled the first time.


Games

Each game lives in its own directory of lisaac/example, with its source code (read the
comment at the top of each file):
- snake, tetris, solitaire, arrow_out, connect_four, break, minesweeper, gems (match three
  gems or more), shooter, startrek.
If you said yes to the games during the installation, they are in lisaac/bin: just type
snake, tetris, solitaire, arrow_out, connect_four, breakout, minesweeper, gems or shooter.


Contents of this directory

- example/   Example programs, from hello_world.li to complete applications.
             example/CodEx/: exercises and examples for teaching, in French.
- lib/       The library, written in Lisaac Ω: collections, strings, numbers, graphics,
             windows, sound, files... lib/teaching/ holds the Logo turtle.
- src/       The full source code of the lisaac compiler.
- elit/      The full source code of the elit editor.
- doc/       The manual (doc/manual.pdf) and slides.
- android/, wasm/, arduino/   Tools for these targets.
- COPYING.txt   The GNU General Public License, version 3.


